 "topics": [
  {
   "topic": "Whether the kernel inspects and withholds secret-bearing service file contents or treats them only according to disclosure class",
   "members": [
    "r1.00",
    "r2.00",
    "r3.00"
   ],
   "runs": [
    1,
    2,
    3
   ],
   "questions": [
    "Does the verbatim-secret withholding rule apply to file response bodies, requiring the kernel to inspect and withhold such files, or are file contents opaque and accessible according only to the file's disclosure class?",
    "When a service answer is a file whose bytes contain a protected credential verbatim, does the kernel inspect and withhold that file, or move it as an ordinary class-protected SpooledFile without recognizing the credential?",
    "When a service returns a file containing a protected secret verbatim, does the kernel inspect and withhold that file, or move it without recognizing the secret and protect it only according to the output's disclosure class?"
   ],
   "units": [
    "context"
   ],
   "id": "T1",
   "judgement": {
    "kind": "judged_before",
    "quotes": [],
    "later_state": null,
    "divergence": "",
    "why": "This asks the same question as P1, and neither the texts nor the passages supporting that judgement have changed.",
    "precedent": {
     "id": "P1",
     "round": "round-44",
     "topic": "Whether service-returned files containing configured secrets are withheld as secret-echoing answers or handled as SpooledFiles under their disclosure class",
     "kind": "answered_later",
     "quotes": [
      "A binding with a `file` output has no other output port and that port\n   is `one` — a proposal otherwise is refused — and the body is that file, whose `Content-Type`, compared on type and subtype without regard to case and\n   ignoring parameters, must equal the port's media type — a response with no\n   `Content-Type` or with two is a `contract_violation`; otherwise the body must be UTF-8 text that\n   parses as one JSON object without duplicate member names — its\n   `Content-Type` is not checked — and each output port takes the member of the same name, which\n   must validate against the port.",
      "Every body as read, whatever its status — reading stops at the ceiling\n   (rule 5) — is searched, before it is parsed or cut, for the bytes of the credential\n   value sent with the request; when they occur, the body is\n   dropped whole and `failure_detail` is exactly `error body withheld: it\n   contained the credential` (A17 rule 2). A 2xx body that echoes it is\n   therefore not used: the answer is `contract_violation` (rule 5), no output\n   is stored or spooled, and for a non-read operation the attempt is `applied`\n   (owner, 2026-10-05: a stored output holding the credential would be the\n   kernel writing it). Only that verbatim echo is\n   searched for; a body carrying the credential in another encoding is kept\n   like any other and is protected only by its class."
     ],
     "why": "State 2 explicitly treats a file output as the response body, searches every response body for the credential bytes, and stores or spools no output when a verbatim echo is found."
    },
    "verified": true,
    "blocking": false
   }
  }
